---
title: Markdown authoring
summary: The prose dialect that markdown() understands: containers, tab groups, fence meta, and diff markers. Zero imports in your content. The compiler injects what a page uses.
related:
  - content/collections
  - content/shell
  - macros/code
---

`markdown()` compiles `.svx` / `.md` with an opinionated dialect on top of CommonMark: admonition containers, markdown-native tabs, a fence pipeline with pluggable stages. Authors write **zero imports**. When a page uses a tab group, the compiler injects the components into the module scope of that page. All content on this page is rendered by the dialect that it documents.

::: tip When to use
This page is for people who *write* content. To wire `markdown()` into your app, see [Content collections](/docs/content/collections). For the `code()` macro, which renders snippets from components, see [code](/docs/macros/code).
:::

## Containers

VitePress-compatible admonitions: `tip`, `warning`, `danger`, `info`, `note`, `caution`, `important`, and the collapsible `details`. Each takes an optional title on the opening line:

```md
::: warning Heads up
Props must be serializable.
:::
```

::: warning Heads up
Props must be serializable. (This box is the example above, rendered.)
:::

Blank lines inside are optional. VitePress-style tight blocks operate. A literal `:::` inside a fenced code block stays untouched. `details` renders as a native `<details>` disclosure:

::: details What it expands into
An `.og-admonition` wrapper that the theme styles. Markdown continues to render inside: code, links, lists, everything.
:::

## Tab groups

There are two authoring syntaxes. Both are rewritten to `<TabGroup>` / `<Tab>`, with the components auto-injected.

**`::: code-group`**: one fence per tab, with the label in `[brackets]` after the language:

````md
::: code-group

```bash [npm]
npm i ogygia
```

```bash [pnpm]
pnpm add ogygia
```

:::
````

::: code-group

```bash [npm]
npm i ogygia
```

```bash [pnpm]
pnpm add ogygia
```

:::

**`::: tabs`**: any markdown per tab, one tab per `== Label` line:

```md
::: tabs
== On the server
Query the brain in a server load.
== In a worker
Ship the static index; the worker filters by scope.
:::
```

Groups with the same labels **sync**: pick pnpm one time, and each install block follows. The choice persists across pages (localStorage). Try it on the group above.

## Fence meta

The infostring after the language flows through the fence pipeline (line highlighting, filenames, [twoslash](/docs/macros/code)). It is the same for a markdown fence and a `code()` call:

- ` ```ts title=src/lib/site.server.ts `: a **filename** in the code-block header, to the left of the copy / permalink actions. This is the common case, and it fills the header so that the header is never empty. When a fence gives no filename, the header falls back to the language name. `title=` reads to the end of the token. Thus paths with `/`, `.`, and `+page`-style route files operate as-is.
- ` ```ts {2-4} `: highlight lines 2–4, with `transformerMetaHighlight` from `@shikijs/transformers` wired.
- ` ```ts twoslash `: type-aware hovers, with the twoslash transformer wired.
- `/// file: app.ts` on the first line: the same filename, but as a magic comment that is stripped from the rendered code (through the `slash_meta()` parser). Prefer `title=`, unless you port content that already uses the comment form.

## Diff markers

There are two dialects for "change this code" prose, shipped as fence-pipeline transformers. **Line-level**: a `+++ ` / `--- ` prefix marks the full line, and the marker never reaches the reader:

````md
```js
--- export let count;
+++ let { count } = $props();
```
````

```js
--- export let count;
+++ let { count } = $props();
```

**Inline**: `+++added+++` / `---removed---` wraps a span *inside* a line. This is the dialect of svelte.dev, and it composes with twoslash:

````md
```js
let +++doubled = $derived(count * 2)+++;
```
````

```js
let +++doubled = $derived(count * 2)+++;
```

Wire them one time in your app config. They are inert on fences that carry no marker:

```ts
import { diff_markers, inline_markers } from 'ogygia/content/markdown';

ogygia({ content: { markdown: { code: { transformers: [diff_markers(), inline_markers()] } } } })
```

::: note Where each dialect is off
Line markers skip languages where a leading `---` is real syntax (`yaml`, `md`, `diff`). Inline markers never treat a `---` alone on its line as a delimiter. Thus frontmatter shown inside a fence renders untouched. For those cases, use `// [!code ++]` comments from `@shikijs/transformers`.
:::

## Extending the pipeline

Each stage above is a **value that you pass**, not a flag that you set. `transformers` speak the ecosystem contract of Shiki (`@shikijs/transformers`, twoslash, the diff markers, your own). `meta` parsers extend the infostring. `variants` turn one authored fence into switchable versions (a JS↔TS converter, package-manager tabs). The defaults are on for the prose features (`containers`, `tabs`, heading ids + anchors, code ids). Each is one key to set off. The full option list lives in the [API reference](/docs/reference/api).

## Do / don't

- **Do** author with the dialect. Tabs and admonitions carry structure that plain prose paragraphs lose.
- **Do** keep install commands in a `code-group`. Readers pick their package manager one time. Each block on the page follows.
- **Don't** hand-import `TabGroup` in a content page. The compiler injects it exactly when the syntax appears. An import of your own only duplicates it.
- **Don't** fake diffs with `diff` fences when you mean "edit this code". The diff language loses real syntax highlighting. The markers keep the Shiki colors and strip themselves.
